    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I've got a heifer here that just came out of a rumenotomy about 6 hours ago. Can you give me a step-by-step protocol for what I need to be watching for over the next few days, specifically regarding the incision site and diet? Also, what are the main signs of internal infection I should look out for? Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Monitor the incision site daily for excessive discharge, swelling, heat, or a foul odor, which could indicate a surgical site infection. Feed the heifer the lightest, most easily digestible diet (like a small amount of pasture or hay squares) and gradually increase as tolerated, avoiding rich grains to prevent rumen acidosis while healing. Watch for internal infection signs such as persistent respiratory distress, noticeable loss of appetite, fever, or a significantly depressed body condition score. If you notice any of these concerning symptoms, contact your veterinarian immediately for a re-evaluation.",
